Title: Add winwrap as a DEP and link it into every final binary
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

winwrap supplies Win32 functions that older Windows releases do not export, so
a binary compiled against a current SDK still loads on them. A statically
imported symbol the target OS does not have is fatal at load, before any
process code runs, so it cannot be fixed at the call site -- which is the whole
reason the library exists. Source and rationale now live in its own repository,
https://github.com/Alex313031/winwrap; this patch is only the two things the
Chromium tree has to know.

It was carried as ~1000 lines of patch until now. Out of tree it can be worked
on without regenerating a patch for every edit, and used by projects that are
not this one -- which it is written to be, the GN build being one integration
rather than the only one.

The dep tracks main for now. Pin a revision before cutting a release that has
to be reproducible from the patch series alone. github.com joins allowed_hosts
because winwrap is the first dependency here not served from googlesource.

Note the condition is checkout_win alone, not 'checkout_win and process_deps':
process_deps is a variable of Electron's DEPS, and this entry is in Chromium's,
where it is undefined.

Linked in through //build/config:common_deps, which BUILD.gn itself calls out
as a dependency of every executable and shared library. That is the right
altitude: the redirect works by satisfying the __imp_<name> slot a dllimport
call site loads through, and every final link resolves those independently, so
putting it on one target would leave the rest importing kernel32. common_deps
is the one place covering executables, shared libraries and loadable modules
together.

The dependency is guarded on is_win as well as use_winwrap. is_win follows
current_os rather than target_os, so without it a cross build from Linux would
hand winwrap to the host tools -- mksnapshot, protoc -- built through the same
group. With use_winwrap off, the default, winwrap_lib is an empty group and
none of this is reached.

Depending on common_deps turns out not to be sufficient on its own, which is
the third hunk. Upstream's solink rules order the linker response file
{{libs}} {{solibs}} {{inputs_newline}}, and link orders it
{{inputs_newline}} {{libs}}. Import libraries are archives and the first one
searched wins a symbol, so winwrap -- a link input -- beats kernel32.lib in an
executable and loses to it in a DLL. The effect was that electron.exe bound to
winwrap while every DLL in the same build kept its kernel32 import and failed
to load, from one dependency that looked correctly placed. Giving DLLs the
same order as executables is what makes the dependency mean the same thing in
both, and inputs-before-libs is the conventional order anyway. Guarded on
use_winwrap so an ordinary build links exactly as before.

diff --git a/DEPS b/DEPS
index 3f29da4cf6..deacbac9fa 100644
--- a/DEPS
+++ b/DEPS
@@ -584,6 +584,7 @@ allowed_hosts = [
   'chrome-internal.googlesource.com',
   'chromium.googlesource.com',
   'dawn.googlesource.com',
+  'github.com',
   'pdfium.googlesource.com',
   'quiche.googlesource.com',
   'skia.googlesource.com',
@@ -1613,6 +1614,19 @@ deps = {
       ],
   },
 
+  # Win32 functions the oldest supported Windows does not export, so a binary
+  # built against a current SDK still loads there. Ours, kept out of tree so it
+  # can be worked on without regenerating a patch for every change, and so it
+  # can be consumed by projects other than this one.
+  #
+  # Tracks main rather than a pinned commit while the library is still being
+  # written; pin a revision here before cutting a release that has to be
+  # reproducible from the patch series alone.
+  'src/third_party/winwrap': {
+      'url': 'https://github.com/Alex313031/winwrap.git' + '@' + 'main',
+      'condition': 'checkout_win',
+  },
+
   'src/third_party/wtl': {
       'dep_type': 'cipd',
       'condition': 'checkout_win',
diff --git a/build/config/BUILD.gn b/build/config/BUILD.gn
--- a/build/config/BUILD.gn
+++ b/build/config/BUILD.gn
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 import("//build/config/rust.gni")
 import("//build/config/sanitizers/sanitizers.gni")
 import("//build/config/ui.gni")
+import("//third_party/winwrap/winwrap.gni")
 if (is_android) {
   import("//build/config/android/abi.gni")
 }
@@ -293,6 +294,22 @@
     # crbug.com/402171653 for context.
     public_deps += [ "//third_party/compiler-rt:atomic" ]
   }
+
+  # Win32 functions the oldest supported Windows does not export. This has to
+  # reach every executable and shared library: the redirect works by satisfying
+  # the __imp_<name> slot a dllimport call site loads through, and every final
+  # link resolves those independently. common_deps is the single place covering
+  # all three link kinds, which is why it goes here and not on any one target.
+  #
+  # Inert unless use_winwrap is set -- winwrap_lib is an empty group then.
+  #
+  # is_win is current_os == "win", not target_os, so this is also what keeps
+  # host-toolchain targets out of it. A cross build from Linux compiles host
+  # tools (mksnapshot, protoc) through this same group, and they must not pull
+  # a library of Win32 implementations.
+  if (use_winwrap && is_win) {
+    public_deps += [ "//third_party/winwrap:winwrap_lib" ]
+  }
 }
 
 # Only the executable template in BUILDCONFIG.gn should reference this.
diff --git a/build/toolchain/win/toolchain.gni b/build/toolchain/win/toolchain.gni
index 5a8e5195d5..3f9c1cbad6 100644
--- a/build/toolchain/win/toolchain.gni
+++ b/build/toolchain/win/toolchain.gni
@@ -11,9 +11,31 @@ import("//build/toolchain/cc_wrapper.gni")
 import("//build/toolchain/rbe.gni")
 import("//build/toolchain/toolchain.gni")
 import("//build/toolchain/win/win_toolchain_data.gni")
+import("//third_party/winwrap/winwrap.gni")
 
 assert(is_win, "Should only be running on Windows")
 
+# Order of the linker response file for DLLs.
+#
+# The upstream solink rules list {{libs}} before {{inputs_newline}}, while
+# link (executables) lists the inputs first. The asymmetry is invisible in an
+# ordinary build, but it decides whether an import library named as a link
+# input can take a symbol away from a system import library named in libs: an
+# archive wins by being searched first, so for a DLL kernel32.lib always wins
+# and for an executable it never does.
+#
+# winwrap depends on being searched first. It supplies __imp_ entries for
+# kernel32 functions that pre-Win7 Windows does not export, and it reaches a
+# target as an ordinary link input (//build/config:common_deps), so under the
+# upstream order every EXE binds to winwrap and every DLL silently keeps its
+# kernel32 import and fails to load. Putting the inputs first for DLLs too is
+# what makes the dependency mean the same thing in both.
+if (use_winwrap) {
+  _dll_rspfile_content = "{{inputs_newline}} {{libs}} {{solibs}} {{ldflags}} {{rlibs}}"
+} else {
+  _dll_rspfile_content = "{{libs}} {{solibs}} {{inputs_newline}} {{ldflags}} {{rlibs}}"
+}
+
 # This tool will is used as a wrapper for various commands below.
 _tool_wrapper_path =
     rebase_path("//build/toolchain/win/tool_wrapper.py", root_build_dir)
@@ -622,8 +644,7 @@ template("msvc_toolchain") {
 
       # The use of inputs_newline is to work around a fixed per-line buffer
       # size in the linker.
-      rspfile_content =
-          "{{libs}} {{solibs}} {{inputs_newline}} {{ldflags}} {{rlibs}}"
+      rspfile_content = _dll_rspfile_content
     }
 
     tool("solink_module") {
@@ -646,8 +667,7 @@ template("msvc_toolchain") {
 
       # The use of inputs_newline is to work around a fixed per-line buffer
       # size in the linker.
-      rspfile_content =
-          "{{libs}} {{solibs}} {{inputs_newline}} {{ldflags}} {{rlibs}}"
+      rspfile_content = _dll_rspfile_content
     }
 
     tool("link") {
